Read everything, SB. The new v10.0.0 (condo-removed) and the production model are, on the evidence here, performance-equivalent — but before this goes into the wiki, three things need to be settled, because two of them will otherwise put a misleading number or claim into an official doc.

Resolve these before the doc is final

1. The aggregate comparison changes two things at once → it's confounded.
Production is scored on 6 lines (016, 032, 072, 078, 088, 090). New is scored on 5 lines (078/condo dropped). So "AUC 0.6549 vs 0.6552" compares two different populations scored by two different models. That's not a clean "did removing condo hurt the model" test. Your own PUP/Condo reference doc got this right — the defensible line there was "when the same policies are scored by both models, AUC is identical (0.655 vs 0.655)." You need that same-population comparison here (both models scoring the identical 5 retained lines) to claim "no degradation" cleanly. If the notebook already does this, point me to it; if not, it's one cell.

Corollary: the new model's slightly higher AP (0.2060 vs 0.2052) is not "better." Condo is a sticky line — removing it raises prevalence, which mechanically lifts the AP floor. Same trap your reference doc flagged ("base-rate artifact... higher prevalence, not better skill"). Don't let anyone read the AP bump as skill.

2. Log loss 2.05 is a bug artifact — do not put it in the doc as a metric.
Both notebooks have y_pred = np.column_stack((y_score, 1 - y_score)). sklearn's log_loss expects column 0 = P(class 0); you've put P(term=1) there, so it's scoring flipped probabilities. That's why log loss reads ~2.05 while every calibration plot sits on the diagonal — those two facts can't coexist otherwise. Corrected log loss is ~0.28–0.33 in both. Fix the column order (or report calibration plots instead of log loss). Reporting 2.05 as a real number invites the wrong conclusion.

3. Scope is condo-only. It's five retained lines, not four.
The new model keeps PUP (088) and drops only Condo (078), leaving 016, 032, 072, 088, 090. Your reference doc's "four remaining lines (016, 032, 072, 090)" language was for a pup+condo removal. Don't carry that phrasing over.

Draft documentation (mirrors your reference structure)

Evaluation of Other Short Retention Model — v10.0.0 (Condo Removed)
Production (train with Condo) vs. v10.0.0 (retrained without Condo)
Executive Summary
Condo (line 078) is migrating to the new repo, after which Classic SPL stops scoring it. v10.0.0 removes Condo from training and retrains on the five retained lines (016, 032, 072, 088, 090). Question: does dropping Condo degrade the model on the lines we keep?
Answer: no measurable degradation. Discrimination is unchanged (AUC 0.655 → 0.655), feature importance is stable, and calibration holds. v10.0.0 is a safe replacement for the retained book. (Pending the same-population confirmation in note 1 below.)
Model Performance

Metric	Production	v10.0.0	Δ
ROC AUC	0.6549	0.6552	+0.0003 (noise)
Average precision	0.2052	0.2060	+0.0008 (composition, not skill)
Calibration (obs vs pred)	On-diagonal	On-diagonal	Equivalent
Eval population	6 lines (incl. 078)	5 lines (excl. 078)	Condo removed

Log loss is omitted: the notebook's column_stack orders the class probabilities backwards, inflating it to ~2.0 in both runs. True value ≈ 0.3; calibration plots are the reliable calibration evidence.
	•	Discrimination: ROC AUC flat at 0.655. AUC is prevalence-independent, so this is the clean read — Condo removal did not change the model's ability to rank churners on the retained lines.
	•	Calibration: Defection-by-year and the nt6 / model_prediction univariates track observed closely in both models. Both slightly over-predict defection at high tenure (years 4–5); behavior is unchanged from production. Small lines (032) are noisier, as expected on low volume.
	•	Composition effect: Removing sticky Condo raises the blended defection rate of the retained book. This lifts average precision mechanically; it is not a skill gain.
Feature Importance
SHAP rankings are consistent across both models. nt6 (6-month renewal count) dominates both, followed by ply_line_cd_enc. Below the top two, total_policy_count and pls_pt_state_cd_enc swap 3rd/4th and drv_prins_years / drv_oldest_driver_increment swap 6th/7th — order noise, not a logic shift. The model's decision structure is stable after retraining.
